direction: right

title: |md
  ## nftables vs iptables
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

ipt: "iptables / x_tables" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  i_body: |md
    **Does:** walks fixed-shape rule entries
    in chain order.

    **Why next rule:** current rule did not
    produce a terminal verdict.

    **State model:** protocol-specific tables
    (`iptables`, `ip6tables`, `arptables`, `ebtables`)
    plus match/target modules.
  |
}

nft: "nftables / nf_tables" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  n_body: |md
    **Does:** evaluates a sequence of expressions
    in `nft_do_chain()`.

    **Why faster for large policies:** sets/maps
    replace long linear chains for membership and
    key-to-verdict decisions.

    **State model:** one syntax across inet, ip,
    ip6, arp, bridge, and netdev families.
  |
}

cmd: "Same policy, different execution" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  c_body: |md
    iptables:
      `-p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT`

    nftables:
      `tcp dport 22 accept`

    **Both:** return a Netfilter verdict at the
    same hook; don't mix engines casually.
  |
}

ipt -> cmd: "linear entry matched"
nft -> cmd: "expression verdict reached"
